        "contact": {
          "url": "https://official-joke-api.appspot.com"
        },
        "description": "This API provides random jokes, making it easy for developers to integrate humor into their applications. With a simple request, you can receive various types of jokes to engage users with lighthearted content.",
        "servers": [
          {
            "description": "Production server",
            "url": "https://official-joke-api.appspot.com"
          }
        ],
        "title": "Joke Delivery API",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "tagline": "Fetch random setup-and-punchline jokes from a free, community-contributed REST API",
        "homepage": "https://official-joke-api.appspot.com",
        "docs_url": "https://github.com/15Dkatz/official_joke_api",
        "license_short": "MIT License (per upstream GitHub repository)",
        "license_md": "- Upstream project [`15Dkatz/official_joke_api`](https://github.com/15Dkatz/official_joke_api) is published under the MIT License.\n- Jokes were contributed by volunteers; the upstream README notes that \"the majority of these jokes were contributed by joke-loving coders around the world.\"\n- No attribution string is mandated by the API itself, but crediting the upstream project is courteous.\n- This SDK is an independent client and is not affiliated with the upstream project.",
        "about_md": "The [Joke Delivery API](https://official-joke-api.appspot.com) is a small, free REST service that returns random jokes in JSON form. It is maintained by [David Katz](https://github.com/15Dkatz) and the open-source contributors of the [`official_joke_api`](https://github.com/15Dkatz/official_joke_api) project, and is hosted on Google App Engine.\n\nWhat you get from the API:\n\n- Single random jokes via `GET /random_joke`\n- Each joke is an object with `id`, `type`, `setup`, and `punchline` fields\n- Joke categories such as `programming` and `general` (the upstream repo also documents `/jokes/:type/random` and `/jokes/:id`)\n\nThe service requires no API key or authentication and CORS is enabled, so it can be called directly from browser code. No official rate limits are published; treat it as a best-effort community endpoint and cache responses where practical.",
        "entity_desc": {
          "random_joke": "A single randomly selected joke returned from `GET /random_joke`, with `setup`, `punchline`, `type`, and `id` fields."
        },
